      "title": "Real-time graphics as a performance medium",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Shared Live Constraint",
          "body": "Both sources treat real-time rendering as more than offline image production: shadercoding becomes a timed public or improvisational performance, while image-based crowd rendering is judged by whether dense animated scenes remain interactive."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Performance Evidence",
          "body": "The shared concept is performance under live constraints. The shadercoding source emphasizes code, image, performer, and audience; the crowd-rendering source emphasizes frame-rate evidence for thousands of moving humans in a virtual city."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Domain Boundary",
          "body": "A topic page should keep the domains distinct: one source concerns artistic live coding, while the other concerns rendering-system engineering, but both rely on technical control to sustain real-time visual experience."
        }
