      "title": "Spatial abstractions for interactive images",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Abstracted Space",
          "body": "The sources share a concern with replacing full physical or geometric complexity with computable spatial abstractions that can still produce convincing images in real time."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Rendering Techniques",
          "body": "In shadercoding, fragment shaders map pixel positions to colors and raymarching reconstructs depth by stepping through distance fields. In crowd rendering, impostors, directional discretization, PCA-adjusted planes, tile grids, and projected shadows approximate people, motion constraints, and lighting cues."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Synthesis Limit",
          "body": "The synthesis is strongest at the level of technique rather than purpose: one source uses mathematical fields and pixels for digital-art imagery, while the other uses image samples and spatial discretization for scalable animated crowds."
        }
      ],
