      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Constraints As Productive Conditions",
          "body": "Both sources frame technical limits as productive rather than merely restrictive. Shadercoding treats mathematical and programming constraints as conditions for artistic creation; crowd rendering turns memory, texture, and model-complexity limits into concrete rendering strategies."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Crowd Rendering Optimizations",
          "body": "The crowd-rendering evidence is operational: sample packing, OpenGL texture compression, compact animation-frame storage, and alpha-channel multipass recoloring reduce memory pressure while preserving or expanding visual variety."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Artistic Comparison Boundary",
          "body": "The shadercoding evidence is conceptual and practice-oriented: mathematical fluency enables original shaders, and mastered technical constraints can become creative motors for digital art. A topic should avoid claiming the crowd-rendering paper makes an artistic argument, but can compare how constraints shape image-making in both cases."
        }
